  "summary": "Health-tech innovation continues across multiple fronts, with AI and wearables emerging as dominant themes. Google is expanding its health capabilities through partnerships with Abbott on AI glucose insights and new Health Guardian features for Pixel Watch and Fitbit, while Samsung's Galaxy Buds received FDA clearance for hearing aid functionality. On the clinical side, major tech vendors like Oracle Health are rolling out AI tools in patient portals, and startups are addressing specific healthcare pain points—Aidoc partnering with twelve health systems on diagnostics and companies developing smart biometric IDs to reduce authentication friction in clinical workflows.\n\nPolicy and regulatory developments are creating significant industry ripples. The Trump administration's new addiction toolkit emphasizes faith-based and abstinence approaches while de-emphasizing harm reduction, and plans to end Medicaid coverage for some gender-affirming care for transgender minors. The administration also drew WHO criticism for its vaccine schedule overhaul order. On litigation, Health Gorilla faces consolidated multidistrict litigation, and Eli Lilly filed six lawsuits against companies selling black-market versions of its obesity drug retatrutide.\n\nScientific breakthroughs and safety concerns are also commanding attention. LSD therapy showed promise for anxiety disorders in a late-stage trial, a rare Prader-Willi syndrome drug faces scrutiny over potential safety issues, and cancer patients are experiencing longer wait times for treatment initiation compared to a decade ago. Health systems are extending care beyond discharge through enhanced technology, while startups like Boulevard Bio and Infinimmune are advancing immunological approaches to disease treatment.",
